WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:02.000
Film Courage: Are we all procrastinators?

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:04.630
Karl Iglesias: Oh, yeah!

00:00:04.630 --> 00:00:05.950
[Laughs] Absolutely.

00:00:05.950 --> 00:00:11.780
We’re probably the biggest procrastinators
around and the only reason for that is because

00:00:11.780 --> 00:00:13.639
writing is hard.

00:00:13.639 --> 00:00:15.420
Creating is hard.

00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:25.810
Yeah…I’m probably the biggest procrastinator
there is…Every writer will relate to the

00:00:25.810 --> 00:00:31.020
experience of trying to do anything else but
write.

00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:34.210
You know, the whole thing about sharpening
your pencils and doing your laundry and cleaning

00:00:34.210 --> 00:00:42.740
your house and you know answering emails or
playing solitaire…on one hand it’s something

00:00:42.740 --> 00:00:48.620
that’s advised because there is that whole
thing about the creative process where we’re

00:00:48.620 --> 00:00:56.040
talking about the gestation period where you’re
supposed to think about your creative problem

00:00:56.040 --> 00:01:00.860
and ignore it and do something else that helps
creatively.

00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:03.180
Some people think “Okay, that means procrastination.

00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:04.339
You can do other things.”

00:01:04.339 --> 00:01:05.560
And there is some truth to it.

00:01:05.560 --> 00:01:11.390
The problem is if you’re constantly afraid
to write and you’re trying to do other things,

00:01:11.390 --> 00:01:14.590
you’ll never write because you’re just
constantly procrastinating.

00:01:14.590 --> 00:01:19.039
So there’s got to be a point where you’ve
got to put that aside and actually write and

00:01:19.039 --> 00:01:20.889
you’ve got to get over it.

00:01:20.889 --> 00:01:26.429
Online there is plenty…I mean there is tons
of advice online about procrastination and

00:01:26.429 --> 00:01:27.750
how to avoid it.

00:01:27.750 --> 00:01:28.750
Because it’s not just for writing.

00:01:28.750 --> 00:01:29.750
It’s for everybody.

00:01:29.750 --> 00:01:31.789
Everybody procrastinates.

00:01:31.789 --> 00:01:37.249
Anything that is worthwhile to do and is scary
to do, people will procrastinate.

00:01:37.249 --> 00:01:42.090
Film Courage: …Well I think you’ve said
that fear is more of what’s at stake, not

00:01:42.090 --> 00:01:43.630
writer’s block and that there really isn’t
writer’s block?

00:01:43.630 --> 00:01:44.630
Karl Iglesias: There isn’t…right.

00:01:44.630 --> 00:01:46.139
At least I do not believe that.

00:01:46.139 --> 00:01:50.069
It could be I’m wrong but I think it’s
fear.

00:01:50.069 --> 00:01:58.869
Fear of success, fear of failure, fear of
being judged negatively, fear of embarrassing

00:01:58.869 --> 00:01:59.869
yourself.

00:01:59.869 --> 00:02:05.499
Those are certainly old fears that I relate
to and that I have had to overcome.

00:02:05.499 --> 00:02:08.700
Film Courage: Why do you think we put the
label of writer’s block?

00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:13.880
Like I’ve heard some people say “Well,
I’m currently blocked right now.”

00:02:13.880 --> 00:02:14.880
Like it’s an affliction.

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:15.880
Karl Iglesias: Because it sounds good.

00:02:15.880 --> 00:02:16.880
Like “Oh, yes.

00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:17.880
I can’t.

00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:18.880
Writer’s block is for real artists.

00:02:18.880 --> 00:02:21.170
I’m a real artist because I have writer’s
block.”

00:02:21.170 --> 00:02:22.879
But a block really just means you’re not
writing.

00:02:22.879 --> 00:02:27.810
It means that the words are not coming up
and the words are not coming up because of

00:02:27.810 --> 00:02:29.840
the tension that prevents you from writing.

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:31.870
That’s what it is.

00:02:31.870 --> 00:02:39.340
So it’s like saying for a plumber…a plumber
never says they have ‘Plumber’s Block,’

00:02:39.340 --> 00:02:40.340
right?

00:02:40.340 --> 00:02:42.170
They just go to do their job.

00:02:42.170 --> 00:02:43.540
It’s about doing your job.

00:02:43.540 --> 00:02:45.000
Showing up at your job.

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:49.611
You have to understand that writing is a job,
whether you get paid or not, you have to show

00:02:49.611 --> 00:02:55.260
up to your desk and you have to put your butt
on the chair and you’ve got to type words.

00:02:55.260 --> 00:02:57.500
So that’s what it is.

00:02:57.500 --> 00:03:03.329
We use it as an excuse, we say it really is
a fear of writing, fear of whatever all those

00:03:03.329 --> 00:03:06.870
procrastinating things I’ve said but it
doesn’t sound good to say “Oh, I’m afraid

00:03:06.870 --> 00:03:07.870
of it.”

00:03:07.870 --> 00:03:12.409
It sounds a lot better to say “I am blocked”
because then people can go “Oh, poor you!

00:03:12.409 --> 00:03:14.890
Yeah, you’re an artist.”

00:03:14.890 --> 00:03:19.670
So it sounds better but it’s really just
fear.

00:03:19.670 --> 00:03:23.099
And again, there is plenty material out there
on how to overcome those fears.

00:03:23.099 --> 00:03:27.970
It’s all psychological stuff we have to
overcome.

00:03:27.970 --> 00:03:32.879
Film Courage: Do you see a lot of that fear
resurface once someone has really gone out

00:03:32.879 --> 00:03:41.569
there and either pitched or shared their material
or had (quote) “people in their peer group”

00:03:41.569 --> 00:03:43.180
give them feedback?

00:03:43.180 --> 00:03:47.870
Karl Iglesias: Yes…and it’s usually because
the writing…[people] didn’t respond to

00:03:47.870 --> 00:03:49.530
the writing as much as they wanted to.

00:03:49.530 --> 00:03:53.620
They really expected the pat on the back and
being told how great it was and chances are

00:03:53.620 --> 00:03:54.620
it wasn’t as good.

00:03:54.620 --> 00:03:56.069
So really it’s up to you.

00:03:56.069 --> 00:03:57.879
It depends on how thick your skin is.

00:03:57.879 --> 00:04:00.099
It’s about how much you want this.

00:04:00.099 --> 00:04:08.159
How much you’re conscious enough to understand
that feedback is important and that you should

00:04:08.159 --> 00:04:09.579
take it as it is.

00:04:09.579 --> 00:04:16.500
You should look at it as constructive criticism
and pay attention to it and re-write it and

00:04:16.500 --> 00:04:17.500
make it better.

00:04:17.500 --> 00:04:18.500
It’s all about making it better.

00:04:18.500 --> 00:04:19.520
You’ve really got to know how to do it.

00:04:19.520 --> 00:04:21.900
It’s a big part of the Hollywood job as
a screenwriter.

00:04:21.900 --> 00:04:26.760
I mean I’ve never heard of something just
being a first draft.

00:04:26.760 --> 00:04:30.330
I mean [you hear] of 30 drafts or 40 drafts.

00:04:30.330 --> 00:04:32.990
So they’re constantly [revising] and you’ve
got to be able to do that.

00:04:32.990 --> 00:04:37.420
If you’re the type of writer who does not
like to be told that something is wrong and

00:04:37.420 --> 00:04:39.570
want to say “No, this is the way it is.

00:04:39.570 --> 00:04:41.230
It’s my art and that is the way it is.”

00:04:41.230 --> 00:04:42.230
You shouldn’t be writing screenplays.

00:04:42.230 --> 00:04:44.110
You should be writing novels or plays.

00:04:44.110 --> 00:04:48.960
That’s really the only two professions where
the writer is king, really.

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:53.630
Unless you’re doing television because the
writer is king there, too.

00:04:53.630 --> 00:04:57.300
But mostly novelists and playwrights where,
yeah the editor can tell you something is

00:04:57.300 --> 00:05:01.720
not working and you can take it or leave it,
but it’s really you that has the final say

00:05:01.720 --> 00:05:02.720
on that.

00:05:02.720 --> 00:05:05.040
Then you should do that.

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:11.240
I think people who have thin skin or are very
sensitive to creative feedback should not

00:05:11.240 --> 00:05:12.240
be screenwriters.

00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:14.780
I mean, they are in for a really frustrating
experience, if that’s the case.

00:05:14.780 --> 00:05:17.270
Question for the Viewers: What are your thoughts
on writer’s block?

